---
import Layout from '~/layouts/PageLayout.astro';
import Hero from '~/components/widgets/Hero.astro';
import Content from '~/components/widgets/Content.astro';
import Features3 from '~/components/widgets/Features3.astro';
import CallToAction from '~/components/widgets/CallToAction.astro';
import RelatedContent from '~/components/widgets/RelatedContent.astro';
import mixinsSlide from '~/assets/images/sass-mixins-slide.jpg';
import { Image } from 'astro:assets';
import bemCode from '~/assets/images/sass-bem-code.png';
import preprocessors from '~/assets/images/sass-preprocessors.png';
import YouTubeEmbed from '~/components/widgets/YouTubeEmbed.astro';

const metadata = {
  title: 'CSS and SASS Student Webinar — OpenClassrooms',
};
---

<Layout metadata={metadata}>
  <Hero tagline="Guest Instructor · CSS · SASS · BEM">
    <Fragment slot="title">
      CSS and SASS —<br />
      <span class="text-accent dark:text-white">OpenClassrooms Student Webinar</span>
    </Fragment>
    <Fragment slot="subtitle">
      Invited to lead a live student webinar for OpenClassrooms covering CSS architecture, SASS fundamentals, and the
      BEM naming convention — giving learners practical, real-world context for the concepts in their course.
    </Fragment>
  </Hero>

  <!-- Context -->
  <Content tagline="The Webinar" title="Bridging course content and real-world practice" isReversed>
    <Fragment slot="image">
      <div class="flex items-center justify-center h-full px-6 py-8">
        <Image
          src={mixinsSlide}
          alt="Mixins slide — CSS and SASS Student Webinar, OpenClassrooms"
          class="w-full rounded-lg shadow-md border border-gray-200 dark:border-gray-700"
        />
      </div>
    </Fragment>
    <Fragment slot="content">
      <p class="mb-4">
        On September 23, 2020, I delivered a live student webinar for OpenClassrooms as part of their <em
          >Produce Maintainable CSS with SASS</em
        > course — a session designed to extend the course content with hands-on explanation, live demos, and direct student
        Q&A.
      </p>
      <p class="mb-4">
        The session ran simultaneously for US learners (8:30AM PST) and European learners (17:30 CEST), reflecting
        OpenClassrooms' global student base.
      </p>
      <p>
        My role was to provide the practitioner perspective — not just explaining what SASS features do, but
        demonstrating how working developers actually use them to write cleaner, more maintainable stylesheets on real
        projects.
      </p>
    </Fragment>
    <Fragment slot="bg">
      <div class="absolute inset-0 section-pattern-b"></div>
    </Fragment>
  </Content>

  <!-- What I Covered -->
  <Features3
    tagline="The Content"
    title="What I covered"
    subtitle="A structured walkthrough of CSS architecture and SASS features grounded in real-world development practice."
    columns={3}
    items={[
      {
        title: 'CSS Structure & the Cascade',
        description:
          'Reviewed how stylesheets cascade from top to bottom, how specificity affects which rules win, and how to think about organizing CSS before introducing a preprocessor.',
        icon: 'tabler:layers-intersect',
      },
      {
        title: 'The 7-1 Pattern',
        description:
          'Introduced the 7-1 architecture pattern for organizing SASS with partials — a widely-used approach for keeping large codebases manageable and scalable.',
        icon: 'tabler:folder',
      },
      {
        title: 'BEM Naming Convention',
        description:
          'Taught Block, Element, Modifier naming with live code examples — showing how BEM creates readable, self-documenting class names that scale across a codebase.',
        icon: 'tabler:tag',
      },
      {
        title: 'Variables',
        description:
          'Demonstrated how SASS variables eliminate magic numbers and hard-coded values — making stylesheets easier to update and maintain over time.',
        icon: 'tabler:variable',
      },
      {
        title: 'Nesting',
        description:
          'Covered SASS nesting — the most widely-used SASS feature — showing how it groups related styles and establishes visual hierarchy that mirrors the HTML structure.',
        icon: 'tabler:indent-increase',
      },
      {
        title: 'Mixins & Functions',
        description:
          'Explained mixins as reusable property bundles and introduced SASS built-in functions for color manipulation, math, and generating consistent design values.',
        icon: 'tabler:puzzle',
      },
    ]}
  />

  <!-- BEM Deep Dive -->
  <Content tagline="Live Demo" title="BEM in action">
    <Fragment slot="image">
      <div class="flex flex-col gap-4 px-6 py-8">
        <Image
          src={preprocessors}
          alt="CSS preprocessors — Sass, Less, and Stylus"
          class="w-full rounded-lg shadow-md border border-gray-200 dark:border-gray-700"
        />
        <Image
          src={bemCode}
          alt="BEM naming convention live code example — .proj-prev__heading"
          class="w-full rounded-lg shadow-md border border-gray-200 dark:border-gray-700"
        />
      </div>
    </Fragment>
    <Fragment slot="content">
      <p class="mb-4">
        One of the session's core topics was placing SASS in context — explaining why CSS preprocessors exist, how SASS
        compares to Less and Stylus, and why SASS became the industry standard.
      </p>
      <p class="mb-4">
        From there, I walked through BEM naming live in the editor — building out a real example with blocks, elements,
        and modifiers so learners could see how abstract naming rules translate into actual class names like <code
          class="bg-gray-100 dark:bg-gray-800 px-1 rounded text-sm">.proj-prev__heading</code
        > and <code class="bg-gray-100 dark:bg-gray-800 px-1 rounded text-sm">.proj-prev--mint</code>.
      </p>
      <p>
        Seeing the naming pattern applied to real code — rather than explained in the abstract — is what makes it click
        for most learners.
      </p>
    </Fragment>
    <Fragment slot="bg">
      <div class="absolute inset-0 section-pattern-d"></div>
    </Fragment>
  </Content>

  <!-- Video -->
  <div class="max-w-4xl mx-auto px-4 md:px-8 py-12 md:py-16">
    <div class="text-center mb-8">
      <p class="text-base text-secondary dark:text-blue-200 font-bold tracking-wide uppercase mb-2">
        Webinar Recording
      </p>
      <h2 class="text-4xl md:text-5xl font-bold tracking-tighter font-heading">Watch the Session</h2>
      <p class="text-lg text-muted mt-4">
        Sample recording of the live CSS and SASS student webinar, September 23, 2020.
      </p>
    </div>
    <YouTubeEmbed id="uZRl5YPO4X0" title="CSS and SASS Student Webinar — OpenClassrooms" />
  </div>

  <!-- Reflection -->
  <Content tagline="Reflection" title="Teaching SASS as a design decision, not just syntax">
    <Fragment slot="image">
      <div class="flex items-center justify-center h-full py-12">
        <div class="text-center">
          <p class="text-8xl font-bold font-heading text-accent leading-none">2020</p>
          <p class="text-xl text-muted mt-3">OpenClassrooms</p>
          <div class="mt-8 space-y-2 text-sm text-muted">
            <p class="font-semibold text-gray-700 dark:text-gray-300">Live Student Webinar</p>
            <p>CSS & SASS · BEM · 7-1 Pattern</p>
            <p>Global Audience — US & Europe</p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </Fragment>
    <Fragment slot="content">
      <p class="mb-4">
        The most important thing I tried to convey in this session wasn't SASS syntax — it was the <em>why</em> behind it.
        Any learner can look up how to declare a variable. What's harder to convey is why consistent naming, modular file
        structure, and reusable patterns matter at the scale of a real project.
      </p>
      <p class="mb-4">
        That's the gap live instruction fills. It's where a practitioner can say: here's a real codebase, here's the
        problem we had before BEM, and here's why the team adopted it.
      </p>
      <div class="mt-6 flex flex-wrap gap-2">
        {
          ['CSS', 'SASS', 'BEM', '7-1 Architecture', 'CSS Preprocessors', 'OpenClassrooms', 'Live Instruction'].map(
            (tag) => (
              <span class="bg-purple-100 dark:bg-purple-900 text-purple-800 dark:text-purple-200 text-sm font-medium px-3 py-1 rounded-full">
                {tag}
              </span>
            )
          )
        }
      </div>
      <p class="text-sm text-muted mt-4">Live student webinar delivered for OpenClassrooms, September 23, 2020.</p>
    </Fragment>
    <Fragment slot="bg">
      <div class="absolute inset-0 section-pattern"></div>
    </Fragment>
  </Content>

  <RelatedContent
    heading="More OpenClassrooms webinars"
    items={[
      {
        tagline: 'Student Webinar',
        title: 'Modern Animations with SCSS/SASS — OpenClassrooms',
        description:
          'A student webinar covering CSS animations with SCSS/SASS — transitions, @keyframes, the 12 principles of animation applied to the web, and Chrome DevTools profiling — for the same OpenClassrooms cohort.',
        href: '/projects/css-animations-webinar',
      },
    ]}
  />

  <CallToAction
    actions={[
      { variant: 'primary', text: 'View All Projects', href: '/projects' },
      { text: 'Get in Touch', href: '/about#contact' },
    ]}
  >
    <Fragment slot="title">Interested in working together?</Fragment>
    <Fragment slot="subtitle"
      >I'm currently available for remote instructional design and eLearning development contracts — let's connect.</Fragment
    >
  </CallToAction>
</Layout>
